front:
**firstElement<T>(arr)**

Write the generic `firstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined`: the first item, or `undefined` for an empty array. Callers must keep the item type and be told it might be missing.

```ts
function firstElement(arr: any[]): any {
  return undefined;
}
```

back:
// Why: T is filled in from the argument (string[] gives T = string). The `| undefined` is
// honest about empty arrays: arr[0] has type T, but at run time it can be undefined.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/generics.html
function firstElement<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
  return arr[0];
}

starter_code:
function firstElement(arr: any[]): any {
  return undefined;
}

type_tests:
const s: string | undefined = firstElement(["a", "b"]);
const n: number | undefined = firstElement([1, 2]);
// @ts-expect-error: the result may be undefined
const definitely: number = firstElement([1]);

front:
**longest<T>(a, b)**

Write `longest(a, b)`: return whichever argument is longer (the first on a tie). It must accept anything with a numeric `length` — strings, arrays — keep the argument type, and reject numbers.

```ts
function longest(a: any, b: any): any {
  return a.length > b.length ? a : b;
}
```

back:
// Why: `T extends { length: number }` lets the body use .length and makes the compiler
// reject arguments without it, while T still remembers the exact type passed in.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/generics.html
function longest<T extends { length: number }>(a: T, b: T): T {
  return b.length > a.length ? b : a;
}

starter_code:
function longest(a: any, b: any): any {
  return a.length > b.length ? a : b;
}

type_tests:
const s: string = longest("a", "bb");
const arr: number[] = longest([1], [2, 3]);
// @ts-expect-error: numbers have no length
longest(10, 100);

front:
**getProperty(obj, key)**

Write `getProperty(obj, key)` so the key must be one of `obj`'s keys and the result has that property's type.

```ts
function getProperty(obj: any, key: string): any {
  return obj[key];
}
```

back:
// Why: keyof T is the union of T's keys ("id" | "name"); K extends it, so only real keys
// are allowed, and T[K] is the type stored under that key.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/keyof-types.html
function getProperty<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

starter_code:
function getProperty(obj: any, key: string): any {
  return obj[key];
}

type_tests:
const userName: string = getProperty({ id: 1, name: "Ada" }, "name");
const id: number = getProperty({ id: 1, name: "Ada" }, "id");
// @ts-expect-error: "age" is not a key
getProperty({ id: 1, name: "Ada" }, "age");

front:
**pluck(items, key)**

Write `pluck(items, key)`: the value of `key` from every item, as an array. Type it so the key must exist on the items and the result has the right element type.

```ts
function pluck(items: any[], key: string): any[] {
  return [];
}
```

back:
// Why: The same keyof pattern as getProperty, applied to every item: T[K][] is "an array
// of whatever is under K".
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/indexed-access-types.html
function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(items: T[], key: K): T[K][] {
  return items.map((item) => item[key]);
}

starter_code:
function pluck(items: any[], key: string): any[] {
  return [];
}

type_tests:
const names: string[] = pluck([{ id: 1, name: "Ada" }], "name");
const ids: number[] = pluck([{ id: 1, name: "Ada" }], "id");
// @ts-expect-error: "age" is not a key of the items
pluck([{ id: 1, name: "Ada" }], "age");
